passage.english:
Nader Chand goes to market by the crooked path.
The girl of the travelling players calls, “Buy me a nose-ring!”
They pick wild fruit from the forest, and the two of them eat;
on a smooth stone the two lie down to sleep.
At night the young lord keeps the girl close to his breast;
by day they rise and wander together, full of many joys.
Holding the lovely girl's hand, he roams from wood to wood;
they pluck the forest's fruit to eat.
Forgotten father, forgotten mother, forgotten house and home;
forgotten country, friends and dear kin.
The two pass day and night with hearts content —
then, without warning, a thunderbolt strikes them.

Love is a fine thing, a kind hard to understand;
Love is something else, not to be likened to lust:
It is one thing, lust another; a wide border lies between them;
Let no one confuse the two! Hear what I tell you!
A lover must be constant, not lewd, foul or faithless;
When parted from the beloved, let him sigh and sigh again,
Let his heart be fixed on one alone, though she be cold or stern;
I hate heartless love: embracing, kissing, noisy smacking of lips.
Let a lover not call this love:
To want one today and another tomorrow, to take parting lightly;
That is like the idle play of boys, of mere youth.
A good lover is one who endures all the world’s trials.

passage.original:
Love gives naught but itself and takes naught but from itself.
Love possesses not nor would it be possessed;
For love is sufficient unto love.
When you love you should not say, “God is in my heart,” but rather, “I am in the heart of God.”
And think not you can direct the course of love, for love, if it finds you worthy, directs your course.
Love has no other desire but to fulfil itself.
But if you love and must needs have desires, let these be your desires:
To melt and be like a running brook that sings its melody to the night.
To know the pain of too much tenderness.
To be wounded by your own understanding of love;
And to bleed willingly and joyfully.
To wake at dawn with a winged heart and give thanks for another day of loving;
To rest at the noon hour and meditate love’s ecstacy;
To return home at eventide with gratitude;
And then to sleep with a prayer for the beloved in your heart and a song of praise upon your lips.

