A longing-bamboo up on the roof,
to be made into a fishing rod;
if you long for me, do not weep:
send word by whoever passes.
A young dove on the platform,
if you are bound for Bandar Rekan:
my longing is more than I can bear;
I send word by every bird.
Eating sugar cane outside the veranda,
a young turtle-dove on the roof ridge;
to meet you for a day and die the next,
I would give a thousand thanks to the Lord.
Hoping, hoping for a puan coconut;
if not a puan, a coconut from Bali;
my heart's hope is set on you alone:
if not you, who else could it be?

The morning star has risen over there,
its home is the Hauran mountains, over there;
by God, faithless wind, turn round, over there,
and fall as dew upon my darling's breast.
He made camp between Yafa and Maʿlul;
she talks with me, the sash of her dress untied;
I am the one made sick by love's parting,
and nothing cures me but the sight of my love.
He made camp between Safita and Akkar;
my cup was clear for me, then it grew cloudy;
if my loves gave me poison, it would be sugar,
sweeter to my heart than any drink.
He made camp between Zahle and Ksaijir;
my darling is neither tall nor short;
we swore an oath: I will never turn from you,
as long as my soul and body hold together.

passage.original:
Amor he hum fogo que arde sem se ver;
He ferida que doe e não se sente;
He hum contentamento descontente;
He dor que desatina sem doer;
He hum não querer mais que bem querer;
He solitario andar por entre a gente;
He hum não contentar-se de contente;
He cuidar que se ganha em se perder;
He hum estar-se preso por vontade;
He servir a quem vence o vencedor;
He hum ter com quem nos mata lealdade.
Mas como causar póde o seu favor
Nos mortaes corações conformidade,
Sendo a si tão contrário o mesmo Amor?

passage.english:
Love is a fire that burns without being seen;
it is a wound that hurts and is not felt;
it is a discontented happiness;
it is a pain that maddens without pain;
it is not wanting more than to want well;
it is walking alone among people;
it is never being content with being content;
it is thinking you gain by losing yourself;
it is being a prisoner by choice;
it is serving the one who defeats the victor;
it is keeping faith with the one who kills us.
But how can its favour bring
agreement into mortal hearts,
when Love is so opposed to itself?

passage.original:
Я вас любил: любовь ещё, быть может,
В душе моей угасла не совсем;
Но пусть она вас больше не тревожит;
Я не хочу печалить вас ничем.
Я вас любил безмолвно, безнадежно,
То робостью, то ревностью томим;
Я вас любил так искренно, так нежно,
Как дай вам Бог любимой быть другим.

passage.english:
I loved you; and perhaps that love has still
not wholly burned out in my soul;
but do not let it trouble you any more;
I would not grieve you in any way.
I loved you without a word, without hope,
worn down now by shyness, now by jealousy;
I loved you so truly and so tenderly
as, God grant, you may be loved by another.

The water at Tanjong Katong is blue,
where the boats of Malacca anchor;
even in the same village I longed for you,
how much more now you are far from my eyes.
If it were not for the key,
I would split the boards of the chest;
if it were not that I would die,
I would open my breast to show you.
A young monkey with mighty arms
goes down to the rice field to eat the grain;
if your love is only half-hearted,
better there were none at all.
Sweet limes in the royal garden,
lost when tied in the corner of a cloth;
sweet words only to my face,
but your heart has gone to another.

My song is a vessel for your name,
O lady of my heart, your name;
in it I'll carry to my Slovene brothers the sweet sound
from sunset to sunrise of your name;
on the vessel, in letters of gold, people will read the glory,
from nation to nation, of your name;
from it a light will still be shining when we two are
across Charon's ferry, the light of your name.
More than Delia, Corinna, Cynthia or Laura,
it would be a loss to forget your name.

On my brocade sash and silk skirt the tear stains gather;
all year, among the fragrant grass, I grieve for my absent lord.
On the jade zither I play through the songs of the south;
rain beats down the pear blossom, and in daylight I shut my gate.

I bought my love with money,
for there was no other for me to have;
sing sweetly, you jarring strings,
sing sweetly of love all the same.
The dream that never came true
was a lovely thing to have as a dream;
for anyone cursed and cast out of Eden,
Eden is still Eden.

Without your face, may the world-burning sun not shine;
without you, may no lamp light up the world;
may no one grow as used to your company as I have;
and may the day I do not see you never come.

Hazel-eyed, coy beauty,
don't treat me like a stranger;
let me be your golden belt,
wind me round your waist.
My heart runs like a racing camel;
a brave lad dies of it by night;
take me to the tip of your lock of hair,
let me swing there like a strand.
Come, girl, stand before me,
ask me how I am;
give me one strand of your hair,
let me smell it as I would a rose.
Karacaoğlan says: what am I to do?
Let me wind your arm round my neck;
coy darling, let me be your slave,
take me as your servant.
